---
import App from '../../layouts/App.astro'
import { href } from '../../lib/nav'
---

<App title="Privacy">
  <article class="privacy">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p>Vector is built so that your study data stays yours. In short: no account, no tracking, no images.</p>

    <h2>The camera</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The camera runs only during a desk session, and only if you turn it on in Settings.</li>
      <li>
        Each frame is analysed on this device by a face model served by Vector itself, then dropped at
        once. No image is ever saved, shown or sent.
      </li>
      <li>
        From each frame Vector keeps a few numbers in memory for a minute at most: whether a face is
        there, the angle of the head, how closed the eyes are, how open the mouth is.
      </li>
      <li>
        At the end of the session only totals are saved: active minutes, seconds per state, number of
        distractions and break suggestions, the focus percentage and a tiredness index.
      </li>
      <li>
        Calibration saves only the average head angle and how much it varies while you read, per study
        station.
      </li>
      <li>Vector never tries to read emotions from your face. The tiredness index is a hint, not a judgement.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Your data</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Everything lives in this browser (IndexedDB): plan, sessions, logbook, settings.</li>
      <li>There is no Vector server, no analytics and no advertising.</li>
      <li>
        The browser itself blocks any connection to other servers (Content Security Policy). The only
        exception is the sync server, used only if you turn sync on.
      </li>
      <li>Backup files are plain JSON, or encrypted on this device when you give a passphrase.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Sync between devices</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Off unless you turn it on with a passphrase.</li>
      <li>
        This device encrypts everything (AES-256-GCM, key derived from your passphrase with 600,000
        rounds of PBKDF2) before sending it. The server, run on Supabase in Frankfurt, keeps one sealed
        blob it cannot read.
      </li>
      <li>
        The server sees only a random-looking identifier, a hash of an access token, the size of the
        blob and when it changed. It never sees the passphrase or the key.
      </li>
      <li>A forgotten passphrase cannot be recovered, by design.</li>
      <li>Turning sync off can delete the sealed copy on the server too.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Deleting</h2>
    <p>
      <a href={href('settings')}>Settings → Delete everything</a> removes all data from this device. Uninstalling
      the app or clearing the site data in the browser does the same.
    </p>
  </article>
</App>

<style>
  .privacy {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-4);
  }

  ul {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: var(--space-5);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
</style>
